A||||5||Two88GTs||Tips on extracting a broken tranny bolt?||||1||
Z||000000||Two88GTs||09-22-2001||04:05 PM||||The car's an 88GT auto, I just recently had changed the tranny filter and gasket and noticed a small trace of tranny fluid on the pan, so I figured I'd tighten the leaking area and @#$%!  The tranny pan bolt head sheared right off.  What would you guys recommend?<p>[This message has been edited by Two88GTs (edited 09-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||09-22-2001||04:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Two88GTs:<BR><B>The car's an 88GT auto, I just recently had changed the tranny filter and gasket and noticed a small trace of tranny fluid on the pan, so I figured I'd tighten the leaking area and @#$%!  The tranny pan bolt head sheared right off.  What would you guys recommend?<P>[This message has been edited by Two88GTs (edited 09-22-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Twos!<BR>I would go to NAPA and get a set of Mechanics Reverse Twist Drill Bits...<BR>When you drill out a bolt in reverse, chances are, the bit will grab and the stub of the bolt will unscrew...<BR>Sence you had the bolt out and back in, it should spin right out...<BR>I had to do the same with a broken starter bolt, and when the bit grabbed, it came right out...<BR>IF it does not come out, you now have a hole for the extractor...<BR>crash...<P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">    <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||09-23-2001||12:16 PM||||Crash is right.  Unless the bolt 'bottomed' out (or topped out in this case), it should be fairly free. If it sheared even with the pan then if you can remove the pan again and get on the bolt w/ visegrips. If you can get at it, then drill it and use an easy out.  Also, if the bolt was that tight in the 1st place then you probably need to hammer the pan holes flat again which means pan removal and new gasket. Be careful not to overtighten because the pan hole will push into the trans an squeeze out the gasket and of course cause a leak. I use a torque wrench as painful as it is, and always pound the holes flat.  I end up with no leaks and no oozing of fluid over time either.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-23-2001||03:03 PM||||The fact a tranny pan bolt broke is rather odd. Usually the threads fail in the aluminum before the steel bolt breaks.<P>Is the remaining head the same as all the others? If someome's forced an odd bolt in there....<P>The extractors might get it, but even then double check the hole carefully. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Two88GTs||09-25-2001||08:13 PM||||Guys, is a screw extractor pretty much a reverse drill bit?  I'm assuming you drill a pilot hole on the bolt with a regular drill bit size recommended for the extractor, and then use the "reverse" setting on the drill while drilling into the bolt with the extractor?  And then back out the extractor counterclockwise?<P>87GTZ34, I just bought a torque wrench so I can avoid mishaps like this in the future, and I'll be changing the gasket with an AC Delco one which has a thin bead of RTV already applied from the factory(the gasket that comes with the Autozone filter+gasket set is cork).||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||09-25-2001||10:04 PM||||Two, and extractor looks a 'bit' like a drill bit (bad pun intended) but you don't use a drill on it.  You can buy/rent a T-handle or use a cresent wrench if it will fit in the space.  You are correct, in that you drill a pilot hole that is deep enough to allow the extractor edges to cut into the bolt hole sides and anchor it so you can un-screw the bolt.  Typically if you buy a single extractor, it will have a recommended drill bit size or come with the bit.  I would still get my hammer out and make sure the bolt openings on the pan are flat and even with the rest of the surface.  Also, I didn't get the GM filter kit and I don't have any pan leaks (yet anyways).  GL.||||reg||1
